/-! # The grouped depth generator against the built one (round par/a-27)

WRITTEN, NOT BUILT. For every declaration on which `#machine_design_svNR f n`
is BUILT in a file of `Tests/AllTests.lean`'s closure, this file generates
`f.machine_composedNRG{n}`, `f.machine_ships_hierNRG{n}`,
`f.machine_design_svNRG{n}` (`Tools/ShippingMachineDepthCommandRG.lean`) and
asserts, per theorem:

* its statement `isDefEq` the statement of the built `…NR{n}` theorem (and
  logs whether the two are the same term);
* standard axioms only.

It does so TWICE per declaration: first with the family facts by the absorb
chain alone (`absorb := true`; the environment is put back afterwards), then
with the default (the old `split` tactic, the chain only where it fails) —
that result is kept. So both ways of proving `hb` / `hv` are exercised on
every declaration, which the sponge needs (see
`docs/parallel/A-depth-grouped.md`).

Logged per declaration (`DEPTH GROUPED …`): the call entries, the groups the
generator formed, the groups the READER's data predicts (distinct (callee,
argument lets) over the sequential entries), the wall time of each variant.
Counts are logged, not asserted, except: the generator never forms FEWER
groups than the reader predicts (that would merge entries with different
argument lets).

## Coverage

| declaration | depth | old endpoint built in |
|---|---|---|
| `ShippingMachineSeqTest.parent`, `.chained` | 1 | `ShippingCallsCloseTest` |
| `ShippingMachineSeqTest.grand` | 2 | `ShippingCallsCloseDeepTest` |
| `ShippingChildBoundaryTest.two` | 1 | `NoPortsTwoTest` |
| `ShippingMachineSeqTest.mid` | 1 | `NoPortsMidTest` |
| `ShippingMachineSeqLoopTest.loopParent` | 1 | `NoPortsLoopParentTest` |
| `ShippingMachineDepthHTest.grandH` | 1, 2 | `ShippingChildBoundaryTest` |
| `ShippingDesignSVTest.mid2` | 1 | `NoPortsKidsMid2Test` |
| `ShippingDesignSVTest.mixD1` | 1, 2 | `ShippingChildBoundaryTest` |
| `ShippingDesignSVTest.topD2` | 2, 3 | `ShippingChildBoundaryTest` |
| `ShippingChildBoundaryTest.mixDeep` | 2 | `ShippingChildBoundaryTest` |
| `MultiInstance.engineOnce` | 1 | `NoPortsSlotlessEngineOnceTest` |
| `MultiInstance.twinCalls` | 1 | `NoPortsSlotlessTwinCallsTest` |
| `MultiInstance.mesh2x2` | 1 | `NoPortsSlotlessMesh2x2Test` |

17 endpoints. All ten imported test files are imports of `Tests/AllTests.lean`
(so they are compatible in one environment). LEFT OUT: nothing that is built
in `AllTests`' closure; the target-only files that run the old command
(`NoPortsSlotlessSeqFamilyTest`, `RemainingProbeTest`, `KeccakProbeTest`,
the sponge probes) repeat `mid`, `loopParent`, `twinCalls`, `mesh2x2` and
the sponge, and are not imported. A declaration whose old theorems are
absent in this environment is LOGGED as left out (`DEPTH GROUPED LEFT OUT`),
not an error — so a wrong row of this table shows in the log.

A child with multi-output calls is generated by the OLD generator here
(`asOld := false`): its theorems exist already. The grouped recursion into
such children (`as_old`) is exercised by the sponge probe only.

## A possible false alarm (read before blaming the generator)

The statement of a depth endpoint depends on which closing theorems of the
children (`c.machine_closes_at`, `c.closes_deep`, `c.machine_closes`) exist
WHEN it is generated (`closesAtNames`, `closesTheorem?`). `preClose` makes
that independent of the order of the user's commands as far as it can
derive them; if a closing of a child was added by a LATER command of the
imported tests, the statement generated here differs from the built one
without any defect of the grouped loop. The error message of a mismatch
names the closing theorems present now.

## For the integrator

TARGET-ONLY for now. It COULD go into `Tests/AllTests.lean` (it imports only
files of its closure and adds only `…NRG…` names, which nothing else
defines); its cost is about twice the depth generation of these 17
endpoints (two variants each), so the recommendation is: target-only until
the old command is switched, then once in `AllTests` with the first variant
removed.

    lake build Tests.Compiler.DepthGroupedFamilyTest
-/

namespace Tests.Compiler.DepthGroupedFamilyTest
open Lean Meta Elab Command

/-- Standard axioms only, an error otherwise. -/
def stdAxiomsG (n : Name) : MetaM Unit := do
  for ax in ← Lean.collectAxioms n do
    unless ax == ``propext || ax == ``Classical.choice || ax == ``Quot.sound do
      throwError "{n} uses a non-standard axiom: {ax}"

/-- The three names of an endpoint at depth `dep`, with the marker `mk`
(`"NR"` or `"NRG"`). -/
def namesOfEndpoint (f : Name) (mk : String) (dep : Nat) : List Name :=
  [f ++ Name.mkSimple s!"machine_composed{mk}{dep}",
   f ++ Name.mkSimple s!"machine_ships_hier{mk}{dep}",
   f ++ Name.mkSimple s!"machine_design_sv{mk}{dep}"]

/-- The closing theorems of `f`'s direct sequential children that exist now
(for the message of a mismatch). -/
def closingsNow (f : Name) : MetaM (List Name) := do
  let r ← Tools.ShippingMachineCommand.readMachine f
  let env ← getEnv
  let kids : List Name := (r.shape.insts.map fun i => i.1).eraseDups
  let mut out : List Name := []
  for c in kids do
    for s in [`machine_closes_at, `closes_deep, `machine_closes] do
      if env.contains (c ++ s) then out := out ++ [c ++ s]
  return out

/-- One variant: generate the three `NRG` theorems, compare their statements
with the built `NR` ones, check the axioms. Returns the log line. -/
def runVariant (f : Name) (dep : Nat) (absorb : Bool) : CommandElabM String := do
  let t0 ← IO.monoMsNow
  Tools.ShippingMachineDepthCommand.preClose f
  let (_, st?) ← liftTermElabM <|
    withOptions (fun o => maxRecDepth.set (Lean.Elab.async.set o false) 1000000) <|
      withTheReader Core.Context (fun c => { c with maxRecDepth := 1000000 })
        (Tools.ShippingMachineDepthCommand.generateDesignNRG f dep false absorb)
  let t1 ← IO.monoMsNow
  let sameTerm ← liftTermElabM <|
    withOptions (fun o => maxRecDepth.set o 1000000) <|
      withTheReader Core.Context (fun c => { c with maxRecDepth := 1000000 }) do
    let mut same : List Bool := []
    for (g, o) in (namesOfEndpoint f "NRG" dep).zip (namesOfEndpoint f "NR" dep) do
      let some (ConstantInfo.thmInfo tg) := (← getEnv).find? g
        | throwError "{g}: not generated as a theorem"
      let some (ConstantInfo.thmInfo told) := (← getEnv).find? o
        | throwError "{o}: not a theorem"
      let syntactic : Bool := tg.type == told.type
      unless syntactic do
        unless ← isDefEq tg.type told.type do
          let cl ← closingsNow f
          throwError "{g}: its statement is NOT the statement of {o} (isDefEq false). Closing theorems of the children present now: {cl} — if one of them was added after {o} was generated, the difference is the environment's, not the grouped loop's (see the header)"
      stdAxiomsG g
      same := same ++ [syntactic]
    pure same
  let how : String := if absorb then "family facts by the absorb chain" else "family facts by split (the chain where it fails)"
  let stLine : String := match st? with
    | some st => s!"{st.entries} call entries ({st.seqEntries} sequential) in {st.groups} groups; absorb chain: Bool family of {st.absorbBools} groups, bit-vector family of {st.absorbBits}"
    | none => "the composed theorem existed (no loop run)"
  return s!"[{how}: {t1 - t0} ms; {stLine}; statements isDefEq the built ones (the same term: {sameTerm}); standard axioms]"

/-- `#depth_grouped_compare f n`: both variants on `f` at depth `n` against
the built `f.machine_*NR{n}`; the absorb-only variant is undone, the default
one is kept. A declaration whose built theorems are absent is logged as left
out. -/
elab "#depth_grouped_compare " d:ident n:num : command => do
  let f ← liftCoreM <| realizeGlobalConstNoOverloadWithInfo d
  let dep : Nat := n.getNat
  let env0 ← getEnv
  let missing : List Name := (namesOfEndpoint f "NR" dep).filter fun o => !env0.contains o
  unless missing.isEmpty do
    logInfo m!"DEPTH GROUPED LEFT OUT {f} at depth {dep}: the built theorems {missing} are not in this environment"
    return
  let clash : List Name := (namesOfEndpoint f "NRG" dep).filter fun g => env0.contains g
  unless clash.isEmpty do
    throwError "{f} at depth {dep}: {clash} exist already (nothing to compare: the generator would return them)"
  -- what the reader predicts: distinct (callee, argument lets) over the sequential entries
  let (nEntries, nSeqR, nGroupsR) ← liftTermElabM do
    let r ← Tools.ShippingMachineCommand.readMachine f
    let seqs := r.shape.insts.filter fun i => (Tools.ShippingMachineMemCommand.memWidths? i.1).isNone
    let groups := (seqs.map fun i => (i.1, i.2.1)).eraseDups
    pure (r.shape.insts.length, seqs.length, groups.length)
  -- variant 1: the absorb chain alone; undone
  let line1 ← runVariant f dep true
  setEnv env0
  -- variant 2: the default; kept
  let line2 ← runVariant f dep false
  let envEnd ← getEnv
  unless envEnd.contains (f ++ Name.mkSimple s!"machine_composedNRG{dep}") do
    throwError "{f}: the kept variant left no composed theorem"
  logInfo m!"DEPTH GROUPED {f} at depth {dep}: reader — {nEntries} call entries, {nSeqR} sequential, {nGroupsR} predicted groups (distinct callee × argument lets). {line1} {line2}"

/-- `#depth_grouped_counts f n entries groups`: the kept composed theorem
exists, and the READER's data of `f` has `entries` call entries in `groups`
(callee, argument lets) groups — an error otherwise (the prediction the note
makes for a declaration, checked on the data the generator groups by). -/
elab "#depth_grouped_counts " d:ident n:num e:num g:num : command => do
  let f ← liftCoreM <| realizeGlobalConstNoOverloadWithInfo d
  let dep : Nat := n.getNat
  unless (← getEnv).contains (f ++ Name.mkSimple s!"machine_composedNRG{dep}") do
    throwError "{f}: no machine_composedNRG{dep}"
  let (nEntries, nGroupsR) ← liftTermElabM do
    let r ← Tools.ShippingMachineCommand.readMachine f
    let seqs := r.shape.insts.filter fun i => (Tools.ShippingMachineMemCommand.memWidths? i.1).isNone
    let groups := (seqs.map fun i => (i.1, i.2.1)).eraseDups
    pure (r.shape.insts.length, groups.length)
  unless nEntries == e.getNat && nGroupsR == g.getNat do
    throwError "{f}: {nEntries} call entries in {nGroupsR} groups by the reader's data; predicted {e.getNat} in {g.getNat}"
  logInfo m!"DEPTH GROUPED COUNTS {f}: {nEntries} call entries in {nGroupsR} groups, as predicted"

end Tests.Compiler.DepthGroupedFamilyTest

/-! ## The general lemmas of the absorb chain: standard axioms -/

run_cmd Lean.Elab.Command.liftTermElabM do
  for n in [``Tools.ShippingMachineDepthCommand.extendBools_absorb,
      ``Tools.ShippingMachineDepthCommand.extendBits_absorb] do
    Tests.Compiler.DepthGroupedFamilyTest.stdAxiomsG n
  Lean.logInfo m!"DEPTH GROUPED: extendBools_absorb, extendBits_absorb — standard axioms"

